ion or subtherapeutic levels [Data: Relationships (6396)]. Therapeutic drug monitoring (TDM) in such populations requires careful interpretation beyond serum concentration values, as organ function significantly influences pharmacokinetics. This contraindication emphasizes the necessity of baseline and ongoing assessment of liver and kidney function in epilepsy patients, particularly when initiating or adjusting AED therapy.\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Perampanel and eslicarbazepine are classified within the antiepileptic drug class\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Both perampanel and eslicarbazepine are formally categorized as antiepileptic drugs, aligning them with established therapeutic guidelines and pharmacological frameworks for epilepsy management [Data: Relationships (56224, 58691)]. Their inclusion in the AED class situates them within a broader context of drugs subject to similar interaction profiles, adverse effect monitoring requirements, and dosing considerations—particularly regarding enzyme induction/inhibition and CNS penetration.\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Drug resistance mechanisms may involve reduced sensitivity to antiepileptic agents\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Reduced drug sensitivity is recognized as a key factor in drug-resistant epilepsy, potentially mediated by overexpression of P-glycoprotein (P-gp) at the blood-brain barrier, which limits CNS drug penetration [Data: Relationships (11999)]. This mechanism contributes to multidrug resistance and may explain why some patients fail to respond to standard AED regimens. Understanding this pathophysiology is critical for selecting alternative agents less affected by efflux transporters or for developing adjunctive strategies to overcome resistance.\"\n        }\n    ],\n    \"rating\": 6.5,\n    \"rating_explanation\": \"The community presents moderate-to-high clinical impact due to the serious adverse effects, contraindications in vul